## What the product does now, by id (one line each)
- Capture: screen + camera + mic + tab/system audio armed together at one epoch; instant start; chips toggle any input mid-take (a real stop and a new segment); pause/resume exist in the engine with no UI (F6); every stop drains every file (P0-tail); a stop past its 5 s budget keeps what reached disk (H5); a device that dies or never delivers is said live and stamped in Recording.lost (H4); an encoder/worker/recorder death closes the segment and opens the next, ≤4 per channel (H1); the disk guard reads real storage growth and auto-stops before the disk fills (B5); the first VideoEncoder per launch is warmed at mount so a take killed at 2 s keeps ~1 s of picture after the first page load (H6), and the same warm METERS this machine AT THE DISPLAY'S OWN SIZE with the take's own codec (B14 2026-09-03) — what a take may attempt at 60 fps is decided from that reading, which carries the frame it was taken at, and a reading taken SMALLER than the surface understates it and may no longer refuse anything; the record press also opens THE ON-TOP PANEL (U1, `?panel=0` off) — a Document Picture-in-Picture window above every other app carrying the timer, the audio ring, Stop and Pause/Continue, closed with the take, absent on any browser without the API.
- Elastic: BIASED UP since E2 (Robert's 09-02 ruling, in the engine). Order of defence, enforced not assumed: (1) the unseen work is shed at `serious` (background brake) → (2) the encoder's byte-bounded burst absorber holds up to 4 frames it used to drop → (3) the picture steps LAST, only at `critical`, only when the take's OWN work is what is critical (`ownLevel` — a machine busy in another window never costs Robert his rate), and only after (1). Up on one confirming sample (~500 ms), widening only for a climb that was undone; a probe at 15 s that no reading may veto, so a wrong reading can never pin a take. Pressure is read PER HARDWARE BLOCK (encoder/cpu/gpu/disk; gpu and disk read `unmeasured`, honestly). Every shed and recovery is in the take (`stopStats.elastic`) and graded (`elastic` dimension). ELASTIC COVERS MAX AND IS SWITCHED OFF THERE, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS MAX NOT HAVING IT — Robert 2026-09-03, correcting this line twice: "elastic must work for max, it is just now turned off so we polish max without it" · "MAX ELASTIC OFF UNTIL I SAY SO" · "until i say so". ONE system, max inside it, the picture step off there until he moves it (`?maxladder=1` inside max, `?floor=1` for M1's floor). Since 2026-09-03 the max STEP actually reaches max MODE (it never did — the slider wrote only `inout.quality.step`), so a max take is no longer refused before it starts and no longer steps mid-take: measured on a max+camera take, before/after on one build, 3 encoders (490 Mpx/s, composite dropping 84 of 307 frames, ladder stepping 60→30 at "pressure critical, encode-latency 365.5 ms", card RED on rate) → 2 encoders (366 Mpx/s, no composite, nothing stepped, card GREEN 13/13). A max take sheds nothing and drops frames where they fall, by his choice and until he says otherwise. M1 BUILT the same system for it behind `?floor=1` (OFF) — the detector reading from the raw screen encoder's own worker through one shared sampler (`capture/pressureSampler.ts`), the unseen work shed first, then the camera's rate, then the screen's 60→30, same `ladderDecision`, back in reverse as the machine eases; measured engaging 841 ms after a load and returning 895 ms after it lifts. The SIZE is its last rung (`?floorres=`, also off: a 5,047 ms seam under load against 30 ms idle — a measurement, not a rule; the order is rate first, resolution after, DECISIONS robert (22)).
- Sync: audio anchored by a min-filter over batch arrivals minus the platform's reported latency (always 10 ms — B7; X14a 2026-09-02 measured what that constant stands in for and it is SHORT on every source: `AudioData.timestamp` puts the true capture 20.6 ms before arrival on tab audio and 41.7 ms on the mic, sd <1 ms, drift <0.4 ms/min, while the reported latency never moves. `metadata().captureTime` is EMPTY on real capturers — `VideoFrame.metadata()` returns {} and AudioData has none — so X14's anchor is the AudioData stamp, not idea 4a's field); video anchored at frame READ; composite origin carried signed so the copied picture is no longer late (B9); wall-clock hold symmetric on the audio a user hears; 987 ms oracle grid (G5).
- Crash: a kill at any moment loses ~2 s (H2); the pending manifest lives in its own IndexedDB with strict durability plus localStorage, and an early keyframe closes the first fragment (H2b) — kills at 2.3-20.3 s all recovered.
- Wedge (Chrome's screen picker never settling on macOS): one press, instant 'stale' refusal, one reload; ladder rung 3 wedged three in a row — page has no move left; second origin inout-kappa-two is the standing per-site test; levers are Robert's (docs/SCREEN_WEDGE.md); the floor rung carries the raw tab-audio flags again — its bare `audio: true` experiment is closed and was voice-processing every take on a machine parked there.
- Editor: framed stage (zoom drag, camera drag, frame presets), split/delete/undo zones, per-clip speed 0.5-2×, Tighten proposals from the capture-time envelope, trims, filmstrip + waveform lanes at 60 fps at any length, edit persisted, keys space/←/→; continue button says "roadmap" (F19); every drag that moves the playhead (ruler, lane, the now-line) is a SCRUB — elements paused, seeks throttled to one a frame; every audio channel is warmed through the OS page cache at any length, holding no memory (191 → 4 ms worst first-play on a 124-minute channel), so nothing starves and nothing waits; opening the editor holds a background render at trickle until the preview is on screen.
- Export: three paths in order — instant packet copy (unedited; the composite at 1080p, or the raw channel when the step matches the take's geometry incl. `max` screen-only: 452 KB instant at 3024x1964) · smart cut (trims/cuts; re-encode only [cut → next keyframe]) · render (worker, 5-6× realtime, decode-bound); prerender makes the file before it is asked for (AT STOP ONLY since J3 — the edit-triggered start is deleted on Robert's ruling; max+camera, paced by pressure, first load shed) — and since J1 an edit no longer throws that work away: the render is chunked 5 s at a time into content-named files and concatenated by packet copy, so an edit costs only the chunks whose pixels moved (2 of 180 on a 15-minute take), an undo costs nothing, and a killed tab resumes at the last complete chunk; exports are persisted background jobs in a dock on every screen, parallel, and RESUMABLE since J1 — a restarted job finds the chunks the dead one finished; size panel = model + probe (probe 1.50× high on still text, B1b); certificate JSON in every file's comment tag; For-AI PDF export (AI1) in a worker.
- Audio: every path mixes the RAW channels; p90 makeup bounded by the p20 floor and the peak licence, u/(1+u) limiter; R128 built and inert until attenuation is allowed; capture-time envelope skips the probe; tab audio raw on every wedge rung.
- Store: OPFS blobs + IndexedDB (`inout` frozen at v2; jobs and the pending manifest in their own DBs); recovery salvages on boot and opens the take; scratch exports stream to disk (4 MB held).
- THE DOOR (M1, 2026-09-03): every change to rate, resolution, quality or which channels run passes through `core/door.ts`, and passing through it is how the change is made — the caller cannot reach the primitive without a ticket only that call can mint. Applied / refused / failed, with what it was measured on and who decided it, into `stopStats.decisions`, mirrored into E2's ledger for the three defence layers, graded by the card's `decisions` dimension, carried into every export's certification as `capture.gaveUp`, and readable mid-take. A test scans the shipped source: `applyConstraints` exists in ONE file, `noteElastic` in two, every `encoder.configure` is an allowlisted entry that says why it is not a dial — a new fallback that bypasses the door fails the build. Chrome's own capture adaptation cannot be owned, so it is DETECTED at 1 Hz and recorded like everything else.
- Report card (S1): thirteen dimensions graded at stop (E2 added `elastic` — did the order of defence hold; M1 added `decisions` — did anything move that nobody decided; G7 added `lateness`), `__inoutReport()` family + `__inoutEditorReport()`, 60-take verdict ring; console only (Robert: bug data is not shown to users).
- Main-thread lateness (G7): every take and every editor open now measures scheduled-vs-actual on the main thread and grades the worst one-second window against Phase 1's own 30 ms claim. The clock is a WORKER and the main thread stamps arrivals — a take runs with the document hidden, where a 16 ms `setInterval` reads 1.2 Hz / p50 983.6 ms late and the worker beat on that same thread reads 63.3 Hz / p50 0.0 ms (measured 2026-09-02). Sampled every 250 ms in capture (0.696 ms/s of main thread, 0.014 above the floor of any worker clock) and every 16 ms in the editor's first 15 s, where it reproduced B10 at 229.3 ms. `?lateness=0` off; rig `scripts/g7-lateness.mjs`.
- Platform: capability × OS gating; Chromium verified; Safari records via MediaRecorder lanes (mic loss P8 blocked on an artifact); iOS cannot screen-record in any browser; cloud UI absent on prod (Supabase env unset); analytics noop in prod; PWA precaches the whole build (a tab across a deploy can record).

